= Trial of Donald Trump

<img src="img/second_trump_impeachment.jpg" style="display:block; width:100%; max-width:500px; height:auto; margin:0 auto 1em auto; object-fit:cover; object-position:top;">

<hr>

President Donald Trump, who has been widely disgraced amongst the American public and even many in his party, was impeached by the House of Representatives on January 13th for inciting an insurrection, and unusually, the Senate is set to vote on whether or not to aquit Donald Trump after his departure from the White House. <span style="color: #DE0100;">Republicans</span> have mostly defered from actually defending Trump, and have instead argued that a motion for impeachment is unconsitutional and unnecessary because Trump does not hold any federal office. Supporters of the impeachment have argued that impeachment is still necessary to bar Trump from ever holding office again, no matter how unlikely it sounds. 

In a 55-45 vote, congress has given the go-ahead to continue with the trial. With the legality of the trial decided, it seems more <span style="color: #DE0100;">Republicans</span> may be willing to cross the aisle, although getting 67 votes unfortunately seems unlikely. Trumps lawyer, Michael van der Veen, has given a defense so poor that many Senate <span style="color: #DE0100;">Republicans</span> who voted against starting the trial are reportedly considering voting aye, including Senator Majority leader Mitch McConnell. Perhaps we will finally put the Trump era to an end.

- @aquitted: The Senate votes to acquit Donald Trump!
- @impeach: The Senate has found Trump guilty of inciting an insurrection


@aquitted
subtitle: A testiment to how far the party of Lincoln has fallen
on-arrival: rural_nsdap += 3; rural_spd -= 2; rural_ddp -= 2; pro_republic -= 3
face-image: img/trump_truman.jpg

The <span style="color: #DE0100;">Republican Party</span> was handed a golden opportunity to finally seperate itself from Donald Trump, who holds a favorability rating in the mid 30's, and they denied it. In a 57-43 vote, Donald Trump has been acquitted by the Senate. <span style="color: #DE0100;">Republican</span> Senate Majority leader Mitch McConnell was allegedly in support of the motion, but was unable to convince enough <span style="color: #DE0100;">Republicans</span> to support impeachment, and thus abandonded the effort. 

{!<img src="img/trump_truman.jpg" style="float:left; width:210px; height:145px; object-fit:cover; object-position:top; margin:0.25em 1.1em 0.5em 0; border:1px solid #888;">!}


While Donald Trump making any sort of political comeback seems impossible (despite the mans delusion), this result is still not a good sign for American democracy and does not bode well for hopes at future <span style="color: #DE0100;">bipar</span><span style="color: #3E88B3;">tisan</span> legislation in the Senate. 

